From 955af1159aec4e9516c97fd30e3d85e2fd1fc906 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tserdeiro <tserdeiro@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 07:56:08 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(git): gate optional commit hooks on their own auto_commit
 key

A fresh project registers all sixteen optional git.commit hooks
unconditionally in .specify/extensions.yml. auto_commit.default ships
false and every event's own auto_commit.<event>.enabled ships false
too (config-template.yml), so every one of those hooks was announced
by core commands as if it might do something -- agreeing to run the
announced /speckit.git.commit was a no-op, since the command itself
checks the same disabled config before deciding not to commit. Command
templates also skipped any hook carrying a non-empty condition
outright, so a condition alone could not have fixed this: nothing
evaluated it.

Give each git.commit hook the condition it needs to respect at run
time: condition: "config.auto_commit.<event>.enabled == 'true' or
(config.auto_commit.<event>.enabled is not set and
config.auto_commit.default == 'true')" -- the per-event key when it is
set, else the shared default, exactly the resolution
auto_commit.py's _parse_auto_commit_config already implements by hand.
Expressing that needs disjunction, so HookExecutor._evaluate_condition
now composes its five atom forms (config.<path> / env.<VAR> with is
set, is not set, ==, !=) with and, or, not, and parentheses: not binds
tightest, then and, then or; keywords are case-insensitive and a
quoted value may itself contain one (e.g. == 'a and b'); an expression
that doesn't fully parse this way -- unbalanced parentheses, an
unrecognized atom, trailing text after a valid atom -- evaluates
False, the same failure default an unrecognized atom always had. The
grammar is a small hand-written tokenizer plus a recursive-descent
parser, standard library only.

Make templates honor the condition: every hook block in
templates/commands/*.md (before_ and after_, ten commands) evaluates a
hook's condition the way HookExecutor._evaluate_condition does -- no
condition means executable; config.<path> is read from the hook's own
.specify/extensions/<extension>/<extension>-config.yml, env.<VAR> from
the process environment, composed the same way -- instead of skipping
any conditioned hook outright. docs/reference/extensions.md documents
the same rule. The two mandatory hooks (git.initialize, git.feature)
are untouched; they carry no auto_commit key to gate on.

HookExecutor.should_execute_hook already evaluates this grammar live
against the extension's own git-config.yml, so turning an event's
auto-commit on later -- through its own key or through the shared
default -- needs no reinstall. One limit remains: a mistyped condition
degrades silently to never-executable.

Tests: a new grammar suite covers composition, precedence (and over
or, not tightest), parentheses, is not set, case-insensitive keywords,
a quoted value containing one, and every malformed shape -- unbalanced
parentheses, an unrecognized atom, trailing text, a missing operand --
evaluating False. The manifest declares the new composite condition on
every git.commit entry and none on the two mandatory hooks; installing
the extension writes it into the registered before_plan entry; and,
across four git-config.yml shapes (default true/false with no
per-event override, and each explicitly overridden the other way),
should_execute_hook and auto_commit.py's own
_parse_auto_commit_config are proven to agree. A parametrized test
over templates/commands/*.md pins that the old skip-unconditionally
sentence is gone and the evaluation rule, composition wording
included, reads identically across all twenty hook blocks.
