+    `_parse_auto_commit_config` resolves `auto_commit.<event>.enabled`,
+    falling back to `auto_commit.default` only when the event's own section
+    is entirely absent from git-config.yml -- an explicit `enabled: false`
+    always wins over the default. The `condition` grammar's `<event>.enabled
+    == 'true' or (<event>.enabled is not set and default == 'true')` encodes
+    that identical rule through `HookExecutor`. Before the composite
+    grammar, the condition only ever checked the per-event key, so a project
+    that turned auto-commit on solely via `auto_commit.default: true` had
+    every hook announced as never-executable even though the script itself
+    would have committed.
+
+    These four configs -- default true/false with no per-event section, and
+    each default explicitly overridden the other way -- exercise every
+    branch of that resolution and pin that both mechanisms agree on all of
+    them.
+    """
